# HAND COPY of the disk-health group in prometheusrule.yaml (this dir) — the drift pin in
# scripts/prometheus-rules-lint.sh asserts expr+for equality per alert name against the CR.
# Re-render with:  devbox run -- yq '.spec.groups[] | select(.name == "disk-health")' prometheusrule.yaml
# Loaded by disk-health.promtool-test.
groups:
- name: disk-health
  rules:
    # ── the drive's own verdict ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    - alert: DiskSmartFailing
      expr: smartctl_device_smart_status{job=~"smartctl-exporter|pve-node"} == 0
      for: 15m
      labels: {severity: critical}
      annotations:
        summary: "{{ $labels.node }} {{ $labels.device }}: SMART overall-health says FAILING"
        description: >-
          The drive's own self-assessment flipped to failed. This is the drive predicting its own death, not a threshold we chose. Identify it (model + serial) with `smartctl_device{node="{{ $labels.node }}",device="{{ $labels.device }}"}`, then docs/runbook.md §Reading a fleet disk's identity and health for the full read. If it carries Longhorn replicas, evict the node's disk BEFORE the drive goes, not after.
    # ── the belt the S4510 class actually needs ────────────────────────────────────────────
    - alert: DiskDefectsGrowing
      expr: |
        (
          max_over_time(smartctl_device_attribute{job=~"smartctl-exporter|pve-node", attribute_value_type="raw", attribute_name=~"Reallocated_Sector_Ct|Reallocated_Event_Count|Current_Pending_Sector|Reported_Uncorrect|Offline_Uncorrectable"}[7d])
          -
          min_over_time(smartctl_device_attribute{job=~"smartctl-exporter|pve-node", attribute_value_type="raw", attribute_name=~"Reallocated_Sector_Ct|Reallocated_Event_Count|Current_Pending_Sector|Reported_Uncorrect|Offline_Uncorrectable"}[7d])
        ) > 0
      for: 1h
      labels: {severity: warning}
      annotations:
        summary: "{{ $labels.node }} {{ $labels.device }}: {{ $labels.attribute_name }} grew by {{ $value }} in 7 d"
        description: >-
          A defect counter that was stable is moving. A non-zero standing count is normal here (drives are bought used, with disclosed defects) — GROWTH is not. Read the drive (docs/runbook.md §Reading a fleet disk's identity and health) and record the numbers in the private hardware register's inventory row before deciding.
    # ── the cable detector: two of this fleet's expensive faults were links, not media ──────
    - alert: DiskCrcErrorsGrowing
      expr: |
        (
          max_over_time(smartctl_device_attribute{job=~"smartctl-exporter|pve-node", attribute_value_type="raw", attribute_name="UDMA_CRC_Error_Count"}[1h])
          -
          min_over_time(smartctl_device_attribute{job=~"smartctl-exporter|pve-node", attribute_value_type="raw", attribute_name="UDMA_CRC_Error_Count"}[1h])
        ) > 0
      for: 0m
      labels: {severity: warning}
      annotations:
        summary: "{{ $labels.node }} {{ $labels.device }}: {{ $value }} new SATA interface CRC error(s) in 1 h"
        description: >-
          Interface CRC errors are the CABLE, not the drive (docs/storage-ledger.md §2026-09-05 — wk-metal-04's SA400 sat on 1,867 of these at a negotiated 3.0 of 6.0 Gb/s, and NCQ retries after them stalled the queue on large writes). Reseat or replace the data cable for this device; do not condemn the drive on this alert alone.
    - alert: DiskInterfaceSpeedDegraded
      expr: |
        smartctl_device_interface_speed{job=~"smartctl-exporter|pve-node", speed_type="current"}
          < ignoring(speed_type)
        smartctl_device_interface_speed{job=~"smartctl-exporter|pve-node", speed_type="max"}
      for: 30m
      labels: {severity: warning}
      annotations:
        summary: "{{ $labels.node }} {{ $labels.device }}: SATA link negotiated below its rating"
        description: >-
          The link came up slower than the drive and port can do — the wk-metal-04 signature, found by hand in 2026-09 only after a 9-hour Garage write job had already paid for it. Pair with DiskCrcErrorsGrowing: CRC errors alongside this means the cable. ⚠ This covers SATA only. NVMe PCIe LANE WIDTH is not exported and is not covered — see FU-284's residual and docs/runbook.md's fit-time check.
    # ── NVMe ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    - alert: NvmeCriticalWarning
      expr: smartctl_device_critical_warning{job=~"smartctl-exporter|pve-node"} > 0
      for: 5m
      labels: {severity: critical}
      annotations:
        summary: "{{ $labels.node }} {{ $labels.device }}: NVMe critical warning bitfield = {{ $value }}"
        description: >-
          The controller raised its own critical-warning flag — spare below threshold, thermal limit, degraded reliability, read-only media, or failed volatile-memory backup. Read the drive for which bit (docs/runbook.md §Reading a fleet disk's identity and health).
    - alert: NvmeSpareBelowThreshold
      expr: smartctl_device_available_spare{job=~"smartctl-exporter|pve-node"} < smartctl_device_available_spare_threshold{job=~"smartctl-exporter|pve-node"}
      for: 15m
      labels: {severity: critical}
      annotations:
        summary: "{{ $labels.node }} {{ $labels.device }}: NVMe available spare {{ $value }}% is below the drive's own threshold"
        description: >-
          The controller has burned through its spare block pool past the point IT calls critical. Plan the replacement now — a drive here is close to going read-only.
    - alert: DiskWearHigh
      expr: smartctl_device_percentage_used{job=~"smartctl-exporter|pve-node"} >= 80
      for: 1h
      labels: {severity: warning}
      annotations:
        summary: "{{ $labels.node }} {{ $labels.device }}: {{ $value }}% of rated endurance used"
        description: >-
          Standardized wear indicator past 80%. Not urgent on its own — plan the replacement into the hardware register rather than reacting. ⚠ Trust THIS field, not a vendor SSD_Life_Left attribute: they disagree on the same drive (Kingston SA400 read 6 vs 5%, and the ADATA SU630's raw field is unusable — docs/runbook.md).
    - alert: DiskMediaErrorsGrowing
      expr: |
        (
          max_over_time(smartctl_device_media_errors{job=~"smartctl-exporter|pve-node"}[24h])
          -
          min_over_time(smartctl_device_media_errors{job=~"smartctl-exporter|pve-node"}[24h])
        ) > 0
      for: 0m
      labels: {severity: warning}
      annotations:
        summary: "{{ $labels.node }} {{ $labels.device }}: {{ $value }} new NVMe media/data-integrity error(s) in 24 h"
        description: >-
          The NVMe controller logged unrecovered data-integrity errors. Unlike wear, this is not a planning signal — check what sits on the drive (Longhorn replicas, a Garage zone) and read the drive before deciding whether to evict.
    # ── the meter's own liveness (PveMetricsAbsent's shape: a belt that cannot say it is blind
    #    is not a belt) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    - alert: DiskHealthMetricsAbsent
      expr: absent(smartctl_devices{job="smartctl-exporter"}) or absent(smartctl_devices{job="pve-node"})
      for: 15m
      labels: {severity: warning}
      annotations:
        summary: "No disk-health metrics from {{ $labels.job }} for 15m — those drives are unwatched"
        description: >-
          smartctl_devices is absent for a whole half of the fleet. If `job=smartctl-exporter` is missing, the DaemonSet is down, the ServiceMonitor lost its target, or the image cannot start — `kubectl -n node-maintenance get ds,pod`. If `job=pve-node` is missing, the hypervisors' smart-textfile-metrics timer has stopped or node_exporter is down — `systemctl status smart-textfile-metrics.timer` on pve and nx-02. While this fires, the drives on that side are not being watched at all.
